L45: 【35†License: CC BY 4.0†info.arxiv.org】\nL46: \nL47: arXiv:2610.37710v1 [cs.AR] 26 Oct 2026\nL48: # zkDuel: Characterizing SumCheck Prover Kernels across GPU Programming Models\nL49: Conference: International Workshop on Hardware and Architectural Support for Security and Privacy 2026; October 31-November 01, 2026; Athens, Greece International Workshop on Hardware and Architectural Support for Security and Privacy 2026 (HASP ’26), October 31-November 01, 2026, Athens, Greece DOI: 【36†10.1145/3857307.3857312†doi.org】ISBN: 979-8-4007-2964-5/2026/10 CCS: Security and privacy Cryptography CCS: Theory of computation Cryptographic protocols CCS: Software and its engineering Software libraries and repositories\nL50: Samarth Narang Affiliation: Tandon School of Engineering, New York University, Brooklyn, NY, USA email: sn2910@nyu.edu , Alhad Daftardar Affiliation: Tandon School of Engineering, New York University, Brooklyn, NY, USA email: ajd9396@nyu.edu and Brandon Reagen Affiliation: Tandon School of Engineering, New York University, Brooklyn, NY, USA email: bjr5@nyu.edu\nL51: \nL52: © cc\nL53: ###### Abstract.\nL54: Zero-knowledge proofs have attracted growing interest from the hardware acceleration community, driven by their use in verifiable computation, privacy-preserving systems, and blockchain applications. A key prover kernel in many such systems is SumCheck, whose table-parallel structure makes it a natural target for GPU acceleration.\nL55: Practical SumCheck kernels over cryptographic fields, however, are shaped not by bandwidth but by multi-limb arithmetic, Montgomery reduction, expression specialization, reduction structure, and launch overhead.\nL56: We present zkDuel, an open-source benchmark that isolates SumCheck as a standalone GPU kernel through matched Triton and CUDA implementations. zkDuel pairs diagnostic microbenchmark polynomials for controlled analysis with zkPHIRE-derived constraints for prover-style stress testing, sweeps 32/64/128/256-bit prime fields, and validates both backends against a CPU reference before any performance measurement.\nL57: Across 800 attempted configurations on an NVIDIA H100, 758 complete with matching Triton and CUDA checksums, and both backends reproduce the CPU reference on every reported workload and field width. On these configurations, we find that the cost of programming-model abstraction reduces with arithmetic width.\nL58: CUDA holds a $3.5\\times$ median advantage over Triton at 32 bits but only $1.4\\times$ at 256 bits, where 30 of 171 validated configurations finish within $1.1\\times$ of CUDA, including 19 large-table configurations on which Triton is faster. An A100 exhibits the same width-dependent convergence, from $4.4\\times$ at 32 bits to $1.6\\times$ at 256 bits. This convergence comes from fixed overheads being amortized as per-element arithmetic grows.\nL59: In particular, host-side launch costs dominate short-running Triton kernels, while Triton’s original block-reduction implementation performs redundant modular additions across threads. Replacing that reduction with tl.reduce brings Triton’s median kernel time to within 8% of CUDA’s on both A100 and H100. Specialized point interpolation accelerates 256-bit evaluation kernels by $1.55$–$1.75\\times$ on both backends.\nL60: At large cryptographic field widths, a Python-embedded DSL is therefore a compelling choice for prover kernels. The remaining performance gap depends strongly on reduction and launch structure, while arithmetic specialization benefits both programming models.\nL61: ###### Keywords:\nL62: \nL63: zero-knowledge proofs, SumCheck, GPU acceleration, finite-field arithmetic, Montgomery multiplication, CUDA, Triton, benchmarking\nL64: \nL65: ^{†}^{†}cc-license: by\nL66: ## 1. Introduction\nL67: Zero-knowledge proofs have moved from a theoretical construction into deployed infrastructure, underpinning verifiable computation, privacy-preserving systems, and blockchain applications. In every such deployment the prover is the bottleneck: it operates on a secret witness and runs orders of magnitude slower than verification. SumCheck is a core prover primitive in polynomial proof systems and plays a central role in Spartan- and HyperPlonk-style constructions (【37†Setty, 2020】; 【38†Chen et al., 2023】).\nL68: Its inner loop is highly regular: evaluate a polynomial over paired table entries, reduce partial sums, derive the next challenge, and fold the table. This structure makes GPUs attractive. A practical GPU implementation, however, is not a simple table scan. For 256-bit fields, every logical field operation expands into limb arithmetic, carry propagation, Montgomery reduction, and temporary storage.\nL69: These engineering details determine whether a kernel behaves like a memory-bandwidth benchmark, an integer-arithmetic benchmark, or a compiler and code-generation stress test.\nL70: Triton has been widely adopted in the machine-learning community as a Python-integrated DSL for writing custom GPU kernels (【39†Tillet et al., 2019】). Its appeal comes from making high-performance GPU programming more accessible than writing low-level CUDA (【40†NVIDIA, 2026】) directly, especially for tensor-oriented floating-point workloads such as tiled matrix operations, reductions, and fused elementwise kernels.\nL71: Cryptographic prover kernels stress different mechanisms: multi-limb integer arithmetic, modular reduction, carry propagation, and layout-sensitive table operations. The question is therefore not whether such a programming model can express finite-field prover kernels, which it can, but what the abstraction costs, and whether that cost stays constant across the arithmetic widths cryptography spans.\nL72: Answering it requires a native CUDA baseline, because only a matched implementation separates algorithmic cost from backend cost. Several lines of work bear on this. Production libraries expose CUDA SumCheck backends (【41†Ingonyama, 2024】), and recent systems accelerate SumCheck within a particular prover by partitioning rounds between GPU and host (【42†Farheen and Tsoutsos, 2026】).\nL73: Complementary work compares Number Theoretic Transform (NTT)- and SumCheck-based primitives under unified architectural budgets (【43†Mo et al., 2026】), and a recent performance study of ZKPs on GPUs reports that prover arithmetic executes on the 32-bit integer pipeline with limited instruction-level parallelism, leaving it bounded by available integer compute; that study covers multi-scalar multiplication (MSM) and NTT (【44†Verma et al., 2025】).\nL74: These efforts target end-to-end prover performance, other kernels, or platform-independent comparison. The question we address is narrower, and to our knowledge, remains open: how does a single prover kernel behave across arithmetic widths and programming models when the proof system, the workload, and the correctness criterion are all held fixed?\nL75: zkDuel closes that gap. It pairs a CUDA implementation with a matched Triton implementation of the same 25 prover constraints, derived from the zkPHIRE constraint set (【45†Daftardar et al., 2026】): Spartan-style satisfiability constraints, Halo2 curve and point-addition, HyperPlonk zero- and permutation-checks, their Jellyfish variants, and polynomial openings.\nL76: These constraints differ in the properties that stress a GPU: product-chain length, number of distinct input tables, polynomial degree, and count of simultaneously live intermediates. That variation is what makes them a benchmark rather than one kernel measured repeatedly. Both backends evaluate identical workload specifications, layouts, and interpolation settings over four Montgomery prime fields: a 32-bit prime, Goldilocks, the Mersenne prime $2^{127}-1$, and the BLS12-381 scalar field.\nL77: Our central finding is that the cost of programming-model abstraction is not a fixed tax but falls steadily as arithmetic widens. On an H100, CUDA holds a $3.50\\times$ median advantage over Triton at 32 bits; at 256 bits the gap falls to $1.37\\times$, with 30 of 171 validated configurations finishing within $1.1\\times$ of CUDA, including 19 Triton wins. An A100 exhibits the same width-dependent convergence.\nL78: We trace this width dependence to fixed costs that dominate when field arithmetic is cheap and are progressively amortized as multi-limb Montgomery work grows. Two effects are particularly important: host-side launch overhead, and Triton’s original block-reduction implementation, in which every thread performs all of the block’s modular additions. Section 【22†5.3】 develops this.\nL79: Two readings follow: Triton is a plausible implementation vehicle for prover kernels at the widths cryptography actually uses, and its Python embedding puts GPU kernel development within reach of implementers who would not write CUDA by hand, which matters because constraint sets evolve faster than hand-written kernels can be rewritten.\nL80: Less expectedly, the result identifies finite-field cryptography as an application domain for a DSL designed around tiled floating-point tensors, a workload class with almost none of the properties Triton was built to exploit. This paper contributes:\nL81:   * •\nL82: \nL83: A characterization of the CUDA–Triton performance gap showing that fixed launch and reduction overheads account for much of the gap at narrow fields and are progressively amortized as multi-limb arithmetic widens. Replacing Triton’s original reduction with tl.reduce reduces the median kernel gap to within 8% of CUDA on both A100 and H100.\nL84: \nL85:   * •\nL86: The finding that programming-model overhead decreases with arithmetic width, measured over 758 validated configurations from an 800-configuration sweep on an H100, with the same trend observed on an A100: CUDA’s median speedup falls from $3.5\\times$ at 32 bits to $1.4\\times$ at 256 bits, where 30 of 171 configurations finish within $1.1\\times$ of CUDA and Triton is faster on 19.\nL87: \nL88:   * •\nL89: Backend-independent kernel optimizations for wide-field SumCheck: specialized point interpolation replaces small-point Montgomery multiplications with cascading modular additions and speeds up 256-bit evaluation kernels by $1.65$–$1.75\\times$ in CUDA and $1.55$–$1.59\\times$ in Triton. A limb-major table layout provides a further 1–5% improvement at 256 bits when combined with specialized interpolation.\nL90: \nL91:   * •\nL92: An open, correctness-gated benchmark artifact with matched CUDA and Triton implementations of 25 prover constraints across four arithmetic widths, available at 【46†https://github.com/snarang181/zk-duel†github.com】. Reported configurations require cross-backend checksum agreement, with both implementations additionally checked against a CPU big-integer reference.\nL93: ## 2. Background\nL94: ### 2.1. SumCheck as a Prover Kernel\nL95: In ZKPs, computations being proven are represented using polynomial constraints that encode the correctness of a program (【37†Setty, 2020】; 【47†Wahby et al., 2018】; 【38†Chen et al., 2023】), a process called arithmetization. The scheme populates constituent polynomials so that their evaluations at step $i$ describe the operation performed at that step, and a composite constraint polynomial vanishes across the execution domain exactly when every step executed correctly (【48†Gabizon et al., 2019】).\nL96: In the implementation, evaluations of the constituent polynomials are represented as input tables. The prover therefore needs a way to show that a polynomial vanishes over the entire execution domain without the verifier inspecting every step directly.\nL97: SumCheck provides one mechanism for this global check and appears in several proof-system constructions (【49†Lund et al., 1992】; 【50†Thaler, 2013】; 【37†Setty, 2020】). It lets a prover $\\mathcal{P}$ convince a verifier $\\mathcal{V}$ that the sum of a multivariate polynomial $f(X_{1},\\ldots,X_{\\mu})$ over the Boolean hypercube equals a claimed value $C$:\nL98: \nL99: (1)  |  | $$C=\\sum_{x_{1},\\ldots,x_{\\mu}\\in\\{0,1\\}}f(x_{1},\\ldots,x_{\\mu}).$$  |\nL100: The protocol runs for $\\mu$ rounds, one per variable $x_{i}$. In each round the prover reduces the multivariate polynomial to a univariate polynomial whose degree is bounded by the polynomial’s individual degree in the current variable $x_{i}$. The verifier checks that evaluating $x_{i}$ at $0$ and $1$ and computing their sum is consistent with the previous claim, then samples a random challenge fixing $x_{i}$ for the next round.\nL101: After all variables are fixed the verifier evaluates $f$ once at a random point.\nL102: From an implementation standpoint, the prover repeatedly evaluates a round polynomial over a table of field elements, derives a challenge, and folds the table for the next round. If the initial table has size $N=2^{r}$, each round halves the active table. Each round views the table as even and odd pairs, and for a pair $(\\mathsf{even},\\mathsf{odd})$ interpolates at a point $p$ as\nL103: \nL104: (2)  |  | $$\\mathsf{interp}(\\mathsf{even},\\mathsf{odd},p)=\\mathsf{even}+(\\mathsf{odd}-\\mathsf{even})\\cdot p.$$  |\nL105: A round evaluates Equation 【51†2】 at $D+1$ points for every constituent input table the workload references, where $D$ is the degree of the round polynomial. Both the table count and $D$ are properties of the constraint being proven, which is why constraints stress a GPU differently.\nL106: This structure makes SumCheck attractive for GPU execution: each round exposes parallel work over many table entries. The kernel is not a simple dense tensor operation, however. Each table entry is a finite-field element, and Equation 【51†2】 is mathematically trivial but expands, at cryptographic widths, into multi-limb arithmetic.\nL107: ### 2.2. Prime-Field and Montgomery Arithmetic\nL108: The workloads in zkDuel operate over prime fields $\\mathbb{F}_{p}$, where the modulus $p$ fixes both the field size $|\\mathbb{F}|=p$ and the number of limbs needed to represent an element. For native-width fields a field element occupies one machine word; for cryptographic widths each element occupies several limbs, and zkDuel’s 256-bit path uses eight 32-bit limbs per element in Montgomery form.\nL109: Montgomery representation makes repeated modular multiplication more efficient by replacing explicit division with multiplications (【52†Montgomery, 1985】). Even so, multiplication remains much heavier than addition: a 256-bit product requires 64 limb-level multiply-add operations, carry propagation across the partial products, and a reduction pass. Addition and subtraction are limb-wise carry and borrow chains followed by a conditional modular correction.\nL110: A SumCheck round compounds these costs along two axes. A single application of Equation 【51†2】 at 256 bits costs a modular subtraction, a Montgomery multiplication, and a modular addition, and a round applies it NUM_INPUTS $\\times(D+1)$ times per pair of entries. Those intermediates are also simultaneously live, resulting in pressure on registers, instruction scheduling, and generated-code quality.\nL111: This is why 256-bit SumCheck is not simply a scaled-up 32-bit benchmark. As field width grows, multi-limb arithmetic increasingly shifts the workload away from memory bandwidth alone and toward arithmetic, reduction, register allocation, memory layout, and code-generation costs. Product-chain structure further amplifies these effects by increasing the amount and lifetime of intermediate state.\nL112: ### 2.3. Arithmetic Width and Soundness\nL113: zkDuel sweeps prime fields at four arithmetic widths: 32, 64, 128, and 256 bits. Small fields are attractive because they make prover arithmetic cheaper, but a single challenge from a 32- or 64-bit field is too small to provide conventional cryptographic soundness levels. Practical proof systems therefore often perform bulk arithmetic over a small base field while drawing soundness-critical challenges from a larger extension field.\nL114: For example, Plonky3 supports Goldilocks and corresponding extension fields, while RISC Zero uses BabyBear with a degree-4 extension (【53†Plonky3 Contributors, 2026】; 【54†RISC Zero, 2026】). This design keeps most prover arithmetic narrow while providing a substantially larger challenge space for soundness-critical checks. zkDuel’s small-width rows characterize that regime, isolating the effect of limb count on GPU behavior while holding the protocol, the workload, and the correctness criterion fixed.\nL115: They use the same generic Montgomery arithmetic as the 256-bit configurations rather than exploiting the special-form reductions available for the Goldilocks prime and $2^{127}-1$. The small-width results therefore vary only the limb count within one arithmetic implementation, rather than modeling a field-specialized small-field prover. The 256-bit rows use the BLS12-381 scalar field and cover the classical setting, where the base field alone carries soundness.\nL116: Two scoping consequences follow. First, zkDuel measures base-field SumCheck only; extension-field challenge arithmetic requires mixed-width kernels and is out of scope. Second, a field in zkDuel is a parameter set rather than a kernel property, consisting of a modulus, a limb count, and a Montgomery constant shared by both backends, so adding a field requires no kernel changes.\nL117: ## 3. Design and Implementation\nL118: ### 3.1. Workload Specifications and Static Kernels\nL119: \nL120: The core abstraction in zkDuel is a workload specification: a backend-independent description of a polynomial constraint. Each specification assigns a stable identifier, records the polynomial expression, and lowers it into term metadata describing which variables appear in each term, how terms are grouped, and which coefficients apply. Both backends consume the same metadata, so a named workload denotes the same computation on either path.\nL121: zkDuel performs no dynamic expression generation in the measured path. Each identifier maps to matched static implementations, which keeps three concerns separate that are easy to conflate: the round count $r$ fixes the initial table size $N=2^{r}$; the field width fixes the number of limbs per element; and the workload fixes which input tables, coefficients, and product chains are evaluated per entry.\nL122: Static kernels make results reproducible, make generated code inspectable, and ensure the benchmark measures kernel execution rather than a runtime expression compiler.\nL123: The suite has two tiers. The diagnostic tier isolates individual expression patterns: a single product, a length-three product chain, repeated input symbols, and independent product terms summed together. It calibrates the infrastructure and attributes behavior to one structural feature at a time.\nL124: The zkPHIRE tier is the primary workload set: 25 constraints adapted from a published SumCheck accelerator (【45†Daftardar et al., 2026】), spanning Spartan-style satisfiability, Halo2 curve and point-addition gates, HyperPlonk zero- and permutation-checks, their Jellyfish variants, and a polynomial opening check.\nL125: The tier is a spread across four structural axes that independently stress a GPU: the number of distinct input tables (2 to 22), the polynomial degree (2 to 7), the length of the longest product chain, and the count of simultaneously live intermediates.\nL126: ### 3.2. Kernel Optimizations\nL127: Both backends implement the same SumCheck round: the active table is paired into even and odd entries, the round polynomial is evaluated at its $D+1$ interpolation points and accumulated, and the table is folded using the round challenge. Holding this structure fixed isolates differences in host-side orchestration and generated kernels rather than differences in the SumCheck algorithm.\nL128: Within this common structure, zkDuel exposes the optimizations below as compile-time controls, each with a matched baseline and optimized path. No runtime dispatch remains in the measured kernels, allowing each optimization to be evaluated independently on identical workloads.\nL129: #### 3.2.1. Specialized Point Interpolation\nL130: \nL131: Interpolating input tables at the round’s evaluation points is the dominant per-element cost. Applying Equation 【51†2】 directly requires a modular subtraction, a Montgomery multiplication, and a modular addition per point per input table, so a workload referencing NUM_INPUTS tables at degree $D$ performs\nL132: \nL133: (3)  |  | $$\\texttt{NUM\\_INPUTS}\\times(D+1)$$  |\nL134: Montgomery multiplications per (even, odd) pair for interpolation alone. At 256 bits each expands into 64 limb multiply-accumulates, carry propagation, and a reduction pass.\nL135: Substituting a compile-time point into Equation 【51†2】 exposes the structure. Writing $d=\\mathsf{odd}-\\mathsf{even}$, the equation becomes $\\mathsf{even}+p\\cdot d$, so $p=0$ returns $\\mathsf{even}$, $p=1$ returns $\\mathsf{odd}$, and each subsequent point exceeds the previous one by exactly $d$. For a point $p\\geq 2$, the multiple $pd$ can therefore be formed from $d$ with $p-1$ modular additions, avoiding multiplication altogether. Figure 【55†1】 shows this for a degree-3 round.\nL136: zkDuel takes the additive path for $2\\leq p\\leq 15$ and the generic Montgomery path beyond, where the chain grows long enough that a multiplication becomes competitive; with a maximum degree of 7 in the suite, the fallback is never taken.\nL137: Figure 1. Interpolating one input table at the four points of a degree-3 round. The difference $d$ is formed once. The generic path then issues a Montgomery multiplication per point, whereas successive points differ by exactly $d$, so the specialized path returns $\\mathsf{even}$ and $\\mathsf{odd}$ directly and reaches the rest by modular addition.\nL138: \nL139: Summing over the points for one (even, odd) pair of one input table replaces $D+1$ Montgomery multiplications with\nL140: (4)  |  | $$\\sum_{p=2}^{D}(p-1)\\;=\\;\\frac{D(D-1)}{2}$$  |\nL141: modular additions. For $p\\geq 2$, both implementations compute the subtraction forming $d$ and the final addition of $\\mathsf{even}$; the specialized cases $p=0$ and $p=1$ omit these operations as well. Table 【56†1】 instantiates this for the degrees present in the suite.\nL142: At $D=7$, the transformation removes all 8 Montgomery multiplications per input table in exchange for 21 modular additions, and on the widest workload, which references 22 tables, it removes 176 multiplications per (even, odd) pair per round.\nL143: Table 1. Interpolation cost per input table per round. Cascading addition removes every Montgomery multiplication at the degrees present in the suite, trading them for modular additions. Counts include the cascading additions used to form $pd$; for $p\\geq 2$, the subtraction forming $d$ and final addition of $\\mathsf{even}$ are common to both implementations.\nL144: Degree $D$  | Mont. mults removed  | Cascading mod. adds\nL145: 1  | 2  | 0\nL146: 2  | 3  | 1\nL147: 3  | 4  | 3\nL148: 5  | 6  | 10\nL149: 7  | 8  | 21\nL150: The optimization is a compile-time specialization in both backends. The point mode and point index are resolved before code generation, so the emitted kernel for $p=0$ contains only the direct return, the kernel for $p=1$ returns the odd value, and kernels for $2\\leq p\\leq 15$ contain the additive chain and no multiplication. No branch survives into the measured code. The generic path remains selectable through --point-mode for comparison.\nL151: #### 3.2.2. Limb-Major Table Layout\nL152: \nL153: A table of $N$ field elements of $L$ limbs each can be stored two ways. The default element-major layout places all limbs of one element contiguously,\nL154: \nL155: (5)  |  | $$\\texttt{addr}=\\texttt{base}+\\texttt{idx}\\cdot L+\\texttt{limb},$$  |\nL156: \nL157: which is natural for scalar arithmetic but places neighbouring lanes $L$ words apart; at $L=8$ that is a 32-byte stride. The limb-major layout stores each limb position as a contiguous plane,\nL158: (6)  |  | $$\\texttt{addr}=\\texttt{base}+\\texttt{limb}\\cdot\\texttt{row\\_stride}+\\texttt{idx},$$  |\nL159: so a single lane gathers one element’s limbs from $L$ planes, but a warp reading the same limb position across 32 consecutive elements touches one contiguous 128-byte region, as shown in Figure 【57†2】. Both backends select the layout at compile time, so the emitted kernel contains only one address computation and no per-access dispatch. The choice is exposed through --layout. Section 【21†5.2】 measures its effect.\nL160: Figure 2. The same four words under both layouts at $L=8$: limb 0 of four consecutive field elements, as read by four consecutive lanes. Element-major scatters them at stride $L$; limb-major makes them contiguous. The pattern extends to a full 32-lane warp.\nL161: ### 3.3. Backend Realization\nL162: The two backends express the same static specialization in their natural idioms. In Triton, the workload identifier, limb count, layout mode, and point mode are constexpr parameters, while round-dependent quantities such as the active table size are passed as runtime values. This distinction is necessary because SumCheck shrinks its table every round, and treating the active size as a specialization key would make the benchmark measure recompilation instead of steady-state execution.\nL163: Benchmark scripts expose TRITON_CACHE_DIR so cold and warm behavior are recorded separately.\nL164: In CUDA, field width is selected through template cases for one, two, four, and eight limbs, and workload identifiers dispatch to matched static evaluators. The CUDA path builds as native executables rather than Python bindings; the benchmark driver launches them, parses their CSV output, and joins rows with the Triton results by workload, bit width, and round count.\nL165: Our CUDA implementation is intended to be a strong compiled baseline rather than a hand-written PTX kernel. We specialize each evaluation kernel to the workload, table count, field width, layout, and interpolation point, which lets the compiler fully unroll the limb loops. Field multiplication uses CIOS Montgomery reduction with 64-bit accumulation, while addition and subtraction use explicit carry chains followed by a conditional correction.\nL166: The code is compiled with nvcc -O3 for the native architecture and otherwise leaves register allocation and instruction selection to the compiler. We swept block sizes from 64 to 512 threads and use 128 throughout, which is either the fastest choice or within 1% of fastest for the evaluation kernels.\nL167: The CUDA and Triton paths therefore perform the same underlying field computation, while retaining three implementation differences: the block reduction (Section 【22†5.3】), modular correction, and the C++ versus Python host loops.\nL168: ## 4. Methodology\nL169: ### 4.1. Correctness Gating\nL170: \nL171: We validate correctness in two complementary ways. Both implementations are first checked against a CPU reference that evaluates each workload polynomial independently using Python big integers. This covers every reported workload and field width at $r=3$ and $r=10$, with fixed and SHA3 challenges; all 381 cases per backend match.\nL172: The performance sweep then carries a checksum over the round-polynomial evaluations produced by each backend. CUDA and Triton results are joined by workload, field width, and round count, and a configuration enters the reported results only when the two checksums agree. Configurations that fail, exceed the 30-minute limit, or exhaust device memory are retained with their status and failure reason, and Section 【19†5】 accounts for these cases in the reported sweep. Figure 【58†3】 summarizes this flow.\nL173: Together, these checks guard against errors in carry propagation, reduction, layout indexing, and challenge generation that could otherwise leave timing results looking entirely plausible while changing the underlying computation.\nL174: Figure 3. Correctness gating in the benchmark harness. Reported configurations require matching CUDA and Triton checksums, while both implementations are independently validated against the CPU big-integer reference.\nL175: ### 4.2. Timing\nL176: \nL177: Unless otherwise stated, all results use specialized interpolation points, the element-major layout, and whole-run timing. Whole-run timing covers one complete SumCheck execution: each round launches one evaluation kernel per interpolation point, reduces the resulting partial sums across multiple kernels, synchronously copies each point result to the host, and folds the table for the next round. Table generation and Montgomery encoding occur outside the timed region.\nL178: Each configuration executes once outside the measurement set to absorb Triton JIT compilation and CUDA module loading, followed by two warm-up runs and ten timed runs. For the H100 sweeps, both backends use synchronized host timers, time.perf_counter for Triton and std::chrono::steady_clock for CUDA, and we report the median of the ten measurements. CUDA kernels use 128 threads per block, while Triton programs use 128 lanes with the default four warps; corresponding kernels use matched grid dimensions.\nL179: Each configuration is limited to 30 minutes.\nL180: For the GPU-kernel measurements in Section 【22†5.3】, Nsight Systems records the duration of every kernel. We sum kernel durations within each SumCheck execution, average this total over the 13 executions of a configuration, and report medians across constraints. Per-configuration kernel times are stable to within 1%, whereas wall-clock time is more sensitive to host state; all wall-clock ratios compared within a table therefore come from the same measurement session.\nL181: ### 4.3. Platform\nL182: Our primary evaluation platform is an NVIDIA H100 PCIe with 80 GB of memory, 114 SMs, and compute capability 9.0, running CUDA 12.9, PyTorch 2.12.0, and Triton 3.7.0. We use the H100 for the main performance results and mechanism studies in Section 【19†5】. To assess portability across GPU generations, we also use two independent A100 systems: an A100-SXM4-80GB platform for a full performance sweep and an A100 PCIe system for GPU-kernel measurements.\nL183: Because host-inclusive timing depends on the surrounding system, wall-clock comparisons are kept within each machine. Table 【59†5】 in Appendix 【34†A】 records the complete hardware and software environments, and the experiment harness captures the associated metadata, logs, and result files for each run.\nL184: ## 5. Evaluation\nL185: We evaluate 800 configurations on the H100, spanning 25 zkPHIRE-derived constraints, four arithmetic widths, and round counts $r\\in\\{14,16,\\ldots,28\\}$ ($r$ also indicates the starting size of each table, $2^{r}$ field elements). Of these, 758 complete with matching Triton and CUDA checksums and form the results below. The remaining configurations are explained by either memory capacity or compilation cost.\nL186: Thirty-six exceed the 80 GB device capacity: each run must hold an input table together with two full-size fold buffers, for a footprint of $3\\times\\mathit{vars}\\times 2^{r}\\times L\\times 4$ bytes. The largest configuration that completes uses 77.3 GB, while the skipped cases would require 90–567 GB.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn4view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view1\",\"id\":85}); \nFailed to fetch https://github.com/Consensys-Incorporated/gnark/pull/1828/commits/e3c2b83773cb9ba345c15be3889b696be99c9a0e: Cache miss--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn4view2 [wordlim: 200] Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn3view2\",\"id\":1}); \nFailed to fetch https://docs.rs/crate/p3-sumcheck/latest/s